<BaseLayout
  title="Bahrain Ports — Khalifa bin Salman Intelligence: China Routing, Transit & Clearance (September 2026)"
  description="Bahrain ports intelligence for China freight: Khalifa bin Salman (BHKBS), the single verified Bahrain seaport, King Fahd Causeway context, China transit windows, 5% duty + 10% VAT clearance and the routing decision."
  canonical={pageUrl}
  ogType="article"
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumb
        items={[
          { label: 'Middle East', href: '/middleeast/' },
          { label: 'Bahrain Ports' },
        ]}
      />
      <span class="badge badge--amber">September 2026 updated</span>
      <h1>Bahrain Ports: Khalifa bin Salman intelligence for China freight</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A single-port intelligence page for shippers routing China freight into Bahrain. This page
        explains why Khalifa bin Salman is the verified Bahrain seaport, how its BHKBS UN/LOCODE fits
        the bill of lading, what the King Fahd Causeway land-bridge means, how long China ocean moves
        take, what the destination cost stack looks like, and the 5% duty plus 10% VAT clearance
        rules that apply at the Bahrain gate.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">
        Confidence badges separate verified port codes and the GCC duty/VAT baseline from indicative
        routing and fee notes. Treat every LOW-confidence port, transit and fee note as a planning
        guide, and verify with the Bahrain port authority or your carrier before relying on it.
      </p>

      <div class="fact-grid mt-3">
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Port</span>
          <span class="fact-value">Khalifa bin Salman <ConfidenceBadge level="HIGH" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">UN/LOCODE</span>
          <span class="fact-value">{khalifaUnlocode} <ConfidenceBadge level="HIGH" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Port type</span>
          <span class="fact-value">Seaport <ConfidenceBadge level="HIGH" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">China sea transit</span>
          <span class="fact-value">Typical 22 days <ConfidenceBadge level="LOW" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Baseline duty</span>
          <span class="fact-value">5% CIF <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">VAT</span>
          <span class="fact-value">10% <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>1. Why Bahrain’s single-port map matters for China freight</h2>
    <p>
      Bahrain is a compact, capital-centric GCC economy, and unlike Saudi Arabia, the UAE, Kuwait or
      Oman, its WorldFreightHub destination map is not a multi-gateway puzzle. The verified data layer
      contains <strong>one Bahrain seaport: Khalifa bin Salman (BHKBS)</strong>. Before any
      China-to-Bahrain consignment is booked, the importer still has a genuine routing question — but
      it is about cargo fit, air-versus-sea and the Saudi land-bridge, not about choosing between two
      equal Bahrain seaports.
    </p>
    <p>
      The commonly quoted commercial gateway is <strong>Khalifa bin Salman</strong>, serving Manama
      and the wider Bahrain hinterland. The verified snapshot does not publish its berths, draft,
      cranes, throughput, operator or fees, so route logically with the single verified port code,
      then request the operational facts from the port authority and carrier. Two names that appear
      in older or competitor content — <strong>Mina Salman</strong> and <strong>Hidd</strong> — are
      not in the WorldFreightHub Bahrain data; treat them as unverified LOW-confidence notes rather
      than a second and third seaport.
    </p>
    <p>
      The statutory side is specific: Bahrain uses the GCC <strong>5% customs duty on CIF value</strong>
      baseline, then adds <strong>10% VAT on the duty-inclusive base</strong>. The real landed-cost
      variance therefore comes from terminal charges, the King Fahd Causeway option, clearance
      discipline and demurrage/detention — the areas this page keeps visible.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>2. Khalifa bin Salman port parameters</h2>
      <p>
        Use this table as an orientation, not a terminal directory. The country, port type, UN/LOCODE
        and GCC duty/VAT baseline are the verified anchors; the role detail, operator, berths and
        throughput are marked LOW because the verified snapshot does not publish Bahrain
        port-authority details.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'parameter', label: 'Parameter' },
          { key: 'value', label: 'Value' },
